      "## The experiment everyone half-remembers",
      "The warning label on rewards comes from a line of research that gets summarised badly. The classic version: children who already loved drawing were split into groups, and some were promised a certificate for drawing. They drew for it. Then the certificates stopped — and that group drew noticeably less than the children who had never been paid at all.",
      "The finding is real and it is narrower than the folklore. It applies to an activity the child already enjoyed. Paying for something already loved reframes it: the child updates from \"I draw because I like it\" to \"I draw because I get something,\" and when the something goes, so does the reason.",
      "That is the whole of the warning, and it contains its own exception. If there is no enjoyment yet, there is nothing to displace. For a child who does not read, a sticker is not corroding an intrinsic motivation — it is buying the repetitions during which one might appear.",
