      "Count sessions. \"We read tonight\" is the unit. It is binary, honest, and cannot be gamed in a way that hurts anybody.",
      "Do not count pages: it rewards thin books and skimming, and a child chasing pages will pick the shortest thing on the shelf every time. Do not count minutes: it rewards sitting near a book with a glazed expression, which is a skill children acquire fast. Do not count books finished: it punishes the child who takes three weeks over something ambitious, which is exactly the behaviour you were hoping for.",
      "Do not, under any circumstances, count difficulty level. Levelled reading targets produce children who choose books to satisfy a label rather than an interest, and the label was always a rough approximation of a text's demands, not a description of a reader.",
      "And keep it private. A chart on the kitchen wall is between the child and the chart. A chart in a classroom is a public ranking, and for every child it motivates near the top it discourages several nearer the bottom, who learn something durable and false about who they are.",
      "## Three ways a family chart fails",
      "The first failure is inflation. The reward that felt exciting in week one is baseline by week four, and the natural response — make it bigger — starts a treadmill nobody wins. The fix is unpredictability. Recognition that arrives sometimes, warmly, resists habituation in a way a scheduled prize cannot.",
