      "The instinct with a second language is to find new material at the right level. The more effective move is usually the opposite: take a story the child already knows completely, and read it in the other language.",
      "The reason is about where the child's attention has to go. In an unfamiliar story in an unfamiliar language, everything is unknown at once — plot and vocabulary and grammar — and comprehension collapses. The child is not learning; they are enduring. In a known story, the plot is already in place. Every unfamiliar word arrives attached to an event the child can already picture, and it can be guessed from the situation rather than translated.",
