      "That load is real, and it is why a first book from an unfamiliar tradition takes a child longer than one from their own. It is also precisely the skill that adult life keeps asking for: holding an unfamiliar frame in mind long enough to understand something inside it, without collapsing it into the familiar.",
      "The interesting part is that the cost drops fast. The second book set in the same place is much easier, the fourth barely costs anything. What began as effort turns into fluency, and a child who has read widely enough stops experiencing unfamiliar settings as difficult at all. That is not a moral achievement. It is a trained capacity, and it is trained by mileage.",
      "## The three axes, and the one families usually miss",
      "Range is worth thinking about along three separate lines, because a shelf can be wide on one and narrow on the others without anyone noticing.",
      "Place is the easiest and the one most often mistaken for the whole job. Stories set beyond your own country, in cities and villages that are treated as ordinary rather than exotic.",
      "Voice is harder and matters more. Who is telling it. A story set in Lagos written by someone who has never been there is a different object from one written by someone who grew up in it, and children pick up the difference long before they can articulate it — usually through the small details that are either right or invented.",
      "Language is the axis families skip. It does not mean only reading in other languages, though that is the strongest version. It also means stories that came from another language, translations that keep their rhythm, and the same tale met in two tongues. A story read in two languages does double duty: comprehension support in the weaker one, and evidence to the child that both languages carry the same magic.",
      "## What this is not",
      "Two failure modes are worth naming, because both are common and both undo the intent.",
